WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Breaking into the Chinese&nbsp;
market is critical to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.580
long-term success of any international company.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.840
Consumer spending in China&nbsp;
is set to double by 2030,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.760
to nearly $13 trillion - the same&nbsp;
amount Americans currently spend.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.340
Yet many foreign firms have a hard time&nbsp;
navigating a market as complex as China.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:25.140
When Amazon entered China in 2004 by&nbsp;
acquiring the Chinese shopping site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:31.440
Joyo.com for about $75 million, it believed it&nbsp;
could replicate its stunning success in America.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.260
Unfortunately for Jeff Bezos, his foray&nbsp;
into East Asia didn’t go as planned.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:42.840
Amazon shut down its Chinese domestic&nbsp;
e-commerce marketplace in 2019.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.520
Yet since Elon Musk set up a&nbsp;
Tesla Gigafactory in Shanghai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.940
demand for his electric cars has been booming.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.940
China is responsible for producing&nbsp;
half of Tesla’s cars worldwide in 2021.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.540
China rocks in my opinion. The energy in China is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.220
great. People there – there’s like&nbsp;
a lot of smart, hard-working people.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:11.220
Selling electric cars and selling everyday&nbsp;
goods are obviously two different industries.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.960
Yet one can argue you CAN make&nbsp;
a comparison. Both are American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.060
companies that tried to make inroads in&nbsp;
China but adopted very different strategies.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.100
Despite entering China early, Amazon&nbsp;
struggled to compete against Tmall.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:31.860
owned by Alibaba Group, which has since grown to&nbsp;
become the country’s leading e-commerce platform.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:39.180
Amazon’s market share fell from 15 percent&nbsp;
in 2011….to less than 1 percent by 2018.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.680
Nor could Amazon China compete with&nbsp;
the other dominant player, JD.com.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.180
Part of the problem was that&nbsp;
Amazon failed to innovate in China.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:53.940
When Alibaba introduced its own payment platform,&nbsp;
Alipay, in which people could pay digitally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:59.940
for products they ordered on Tmall, Amazon was&nbsp;
still accepting cash from buyers upon delivery.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.500
Amazon also failed to understand&nbsp;
what appealed to Chinese shoppers.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:10.680
Alibaba’s annual shopping extravaganza&nbsp;
Singles Day was a huge hit. Amazon didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.940
have a counter as its annual discounted&nbsp;
event Prime Day didn’t exist yet.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.980
The websites of Tmall and JD were&nbsp;
also very colorful and festive.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.660
Amazon’s website looked rather bland -&nbsp;
mirroring its other home pages around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.580
the world. Americans prefer a minimalist&nbsp;
design, which is not the case in China.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.920
It didn’t help that Amazon China relied&nbsp;
on support from company headquarters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.100
Seattle and was slow to respond to market signals.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.680
The company also turned off Chinese sellers.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.660
Sellers were used to paying 2% to 5%&nbsp;
of their sales to Alibaba in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.320
to paying for ads to make their&nbsp;
listings more prominent. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.520
Amazon executives were not a fan of&nbsp;
the advertising model and instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.940
charged Chinese sellers more, 10% to 15% of&nbsp;
sales. So naturally, they flocked to Alibaba.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.620
Perhaps the biggest mistake Jeff Bezos&nbsp;
made in China…was that he failed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.280
understand the importance of forging&nbsp;
good relations with the government.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.500
As Brad Stone writes in his book about&nbsp;
Amazon’s growth called Amazon Unbound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:19.920
“Amazon.cn executives from that time said&nbsp;
that Bezos was totally uninterested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.300
understanding the inner machinations of&nbsp;
the Chinese government, cultivating ties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.400
with Chinese leaders, or using his budding&nbsp;
fame to help Amazon’s cause in the country…”

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:37.140
Elon Musk didn’t make the same mistake when he&nbsp;
opened Tesla’s first factory in China in 2019.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.860
Musk has consistently made an&nbsp;
effort to woo Chinese officials.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:46.140
Taking China’s ambassador to the U.S. for a&nbsp;
drive in a Model S Plaid - which the ambassador&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:52.260
described as a “smooth ride”. It’s unclear if they&nbsp;
tried plaid mode - the fastest acceleration mode.&nbsp;

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.800
On his Weibo account, Musk frequently praises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.580
China’s space program and economic&nbsp;
growth over the past three decades.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.360
The Chinese government has&nbsp;
been very supportive of Tesla.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.240
In 2019, Tesla became the first foreign&nbsp;
automaker to open an independent factory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.040
in China. Normally, foreign carmakers&nbsp;
must team up with a Chinese partner.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:20.280
There apparently was no excessive red tape as&nbsp;
Tesla built its Shanghai Gigafactory in record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:27.420
time: going from permits to a finished plant&nbsp;
in just 168 working days or about six months!

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:32.640
Tesla was one of 600 businesses that the&nbsp;
Chinese government deemed important enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.140
to open first amid the lockdown&nbsp;
in Shanghai in the spring of 2022.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.120
Authorities arranged buses to transport&nbsp;
thousands of Tesla workers and helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.020
carry out the required disinfection&nbsp;
to curb the spread of COVID-19.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:51.660
Musk has heaped praise on Chinese workers, like&nbsp;
in this interview with the Financial Times.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.860
There’s just a lot of super talented,&nbsp;
hardworking people in China that are who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.820
strongly believe in manufacturing. They&nbsp;
won’t just be burning the midnight oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:12.180
they’ll be burning the 3 a.m. oil. They&nbsp;
won’t even leave the factory type of thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.900
whereas in America, people are&nbsp;
trying to avoid going to work at all.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.500
While Amazon China failed to&nbsp;
get close to Chinese consumers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:25.440
the minimalist website being one example, Musk&nbsp;
has really tried to embrace Chinese culture.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.640
When he posted an ancient Chinese poem&nbsp;
about the importance of getting along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:34.260
on his social media accounts, it went&nbsp;
viral, as people discussed what he meant.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:41.340
While Amazon China relied on Seattle for support,&nbsp;
Tesla China is not simply a branch of Tesla.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.640
It’s set up to do original&nbsp;
research and development work.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.120
Tesla opened a research center and a separate data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.560
center in Shanghai in 2021. Both&nbsp;
are located near the Gigafactory.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.120
Musk’s push for EV development&nbsp;
conveniently aligns with Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.980
president Xi Jinping’s pledge to make&nbsp;
China the leader in electric innovation.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:07.680
Beijing aims to have 40 percent of&nbsp;
vehicles sold in China be electric by 2030.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.340
And Elon Musk is the one&nbsp;
person who can get them there.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:16.680
It’s no wonder Beijing has been showering&nbsp;
Tesla with goodies such as cheap land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:22.560
low-interest loans, and tax incentives -&nbsp;
exempting Tesla cars from a 10% purchase tax.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:29.340
Within three years of opening, Giga&nbsp;
Shanghai is now on track to produce 900,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:33.300
cars a year if recent monthly&nbsp;
deliveries are any indication.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.140
And it’s building another assembly&nbsp;
line near its existing plant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.940
which could add another half a&nbsp;
million Model 3s and Model Ys a year.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.640
According to Tesla, this will turn Shanghai&nbsp;
into "the world's largest vehicle export hub".

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:52.620
Musk has remained popular in China&nbsp;
despite deteriorating relations with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.120
the U.S. I’ve made a series of videos&nbsp;
recently on how Washington is trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.960
keep the world’s most critical chip&nbsp;
technology out of Beijing’s hands.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.820
There’s also the issue of large-scale&nbsp;
human rights abuses in the country.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.400
So, it’s a delicate balance for Musk to&nbsp;
straddle the geopolitical fault lines.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.800
But Elon Musk needs China.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.580
Chinese sales helped propel&nbsp;
Tesla to its first full year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:21.420
of profitability in 2020 - 18 years in the making.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.120
And China needs Elon Musk.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:29.820
Chinese leaders have reportedly grown frustrated&nbsp;
with the performance of domestic electric vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:34.380
companies, and believe Tesla can lead&nbsp;
the transition to an electric future.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.740
Although, Chinese companies&nbsp;
are starting to catch up.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:43.020
In the third quarter of 2022, BYD sold 537,164&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:47.940
new energy vehicles compared&nbsp;
to Tesla’s 343,830 vehicles.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:53.820
However, only about half of BYD’s&nbsp;
car sold were fully electric as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:59.040
new energy vehicles also include plug-in&nbsp;
hybrids and hydrogen fuel-cell vehicles.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.000
So Tesla is still ahead in&nbsp;
all-electric vehicle sales.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.260
For now, Tesla is enjoying a fate that is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.740
different from Amazon’s in the&nbsp;
world’s most populous country.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:17.400
Tesla’s success lies in its ability to innovate&nbsp;
quickly. As Musk has said time and time again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.100
what matters is the pace of innovation.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.820
Well, another company is&nbsp;
innovating an everyday product.

